integer values for all i=0..3, α must be integer. Then β must be integer as well (since f(0)=β must be integer). So lines with integer slopes (except 0 and -1) and integer intercepts produce integer points across all columns. However, we also need the values to be within the allowed range: 1 ≤ f(i) ≤ i+1.\n\nLet's try integer slopes.\n\n- α = 1: f(i) = i + β. For i=0, f(0)=β must be 1 (since only row 1). So β=1. Then f(i) = i+1. For i=0 =>1, i=1=>2, i=2=>3, i=3=>4. These are exactly the maximum row in each column (i+1). So line with slope 1 and intercept 1 passes through points (n-i, i+1) i.e., (n,1), (n-1,2), (n-2,3), (n-3,4). That's a sunny line (slope 1). Good. So L1: slope 1, passes through the topmost points of each column.\n\n- α = 2: f(i) = 2i + β. For i=0, f(0)=β must be 1 (since row 1). So β=1. Then f(i) = 2i+1. For i=0 =>1, i=1=>3, i=2=>5, i=3=>7. But allowed rows: column i=1 has rows 1..2, so f(1)=3 >2, not allowed. So line with slope 2 cannot have a point in column i=1. But we could have line that doesn't include column i=1 (skip). That's allowed: a line can skip some columns. So we could have line with slope 2 that includes points for i=0,2 maybe? Let's check: For i=0 =>1 (valid). For i=2 =>5, but column i=2 has rows up to 3, so 5 >3, not allowed. So slope 2 line cannot have any point beyond i=0. So not useful.\n\n- α = -2: f(i) = -2i + β. For i=0, β=1 => f(i) = 1 - 2i. For i=0 =>1, i=1 => -1 (invalid). So line only covers i=0.\n\n- α = -3: f(i) = -3i + β, β=1 => f(1) = -2 (invalid). So not good.\n\n- α = -2 maybe with different β? For i=0, we need f(0) = β = some row in column 0 (only row 1). So β must be 1. So slope -2 line only covers i=0.\n\nThus the only integer slope that works for multiple columns is α = 1 (slope 1). Also α = -1 is disallowed. α = 0 is disallowed. So integer slopes other than 1 produce invalid points for i≥1.\n\nThus we need to consider rational slopes with denominator >1 to get integer values for some columns but not all. For example, α = -1/2 gave integer values for i even (i=0,2,4,...). Indeed f(i) = 1 - i/2 yields integer for even i. So for m=4, we have i=0,2 even, i=1,3 odd. So we can have lines that cover only even columns or only odd columns.\n\nThus we can use slopes with denominator 2 to cover even columns. Similarly slopes with denominator 3 can cover columns where i is multiple of 3, etc.\n\nThus we can design lines that cover subsets of columns.\n\nGoal: cover all points in columns i=0..3. Let's list points by column:\n\ni=0: rows {1}\ni=1: rows {1,2}\ni=2: rows {1,2,3}\ni=3: rows {1,2,3,4}\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line.\n\nWe can try to use lines with slopes:\n\n- α = 1 (slope 1) covering points (i, i+1) for i=0..3 (topmost points). That covers points: (0,1), (1,2), (2,3), (3,4). So we have covered (n,1), (n-1,2), (n-2,3), (n-3,4). Good.\n\nNow remaining points:\n\ni=0: none left (since only row 1 covered)\ni=1: row 1 left (point (n-1,1))\ni=2: rows 1,2 left (points (n-2,1),(n-2,2))\ni=3: rows 1,2,3 left (points (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3))\n\nNow we need to cover these 6 points with 3 remaining lines.\n\nWe can try to use a line with slope -1/2 covering even columns i=0,2. But i=0 already covered, but we can still use it for i=2 maybe. Let's see: slope -1/2, β such that f(2) = some integer row in column 2. For i=2, we need f(2) ∈ {1,2}. Let's set f(2) = 2 maybe. Then f(i) = β - i/2. So f(2) = β - 1 = 2 => β = 3. Then f(0) = 3 (but column 0 only has row 1). So line would also have a point at i=0 row 3, which is invalid. But we could ignore i=0 (line can have a point there but it's not in T_n). However, the line would intersect column 0 at row 3, which is not a point we need to cover. That's okay; we don't need to cover that point. The line can intersect column 0 at a point not in T_n; that's fine. The line just needs to cover the required points; it may also intersect other points not in T_n, but that's okay. So we can have line L2: slope -1/2, β = 3, equation y = -0.5 x + b? Let's compute in terms of i: f(i) = 3 - i/2. For i=2 => f=2 (good). For i=0 => f=3 (point (n,3) not in T_n). For i=1 => f=2.5 (not integer). For i=3 => f=1.5 (not integer). So L2 covers only point (i=2, r=2) i.e., (n-2,2). That's not enough; we need to cover more points.\n\nWe can try slope -1/2 with β = 2: f(i) = 2 - i/2. Then f(2) = 1 (covers (n-2,1)). f(0) = 2 (invalid). So L2 covers (n-2,1). That's one point.\n\nWe need lines that cover multiple points across columns.\n\nConsider slope -2 (α = -2). For i=1, f(1) = β - 2. To be integer within [1,2], we need β = 3 or 4. Let's try β = 3: f(i) = 3 - 2i. Then f(0)=3 (invalid), f(1)=1 (covers (n-1,1)), f(2) = -1 (invalid), f(3) = -3 (invalid). So L3 covers (n-1,1) only.\n\nConsider slope 2 (α = 2). For i=1, f(1) = β + 2. To be ≤2, β must be ≤0, but β must be integer. β=0 gives f(1)=2 (covers (n-1,2) but that's already covered by L1). β=-1 gives f(1)=1 (covers (n-1,1)). But then f(0) = -1 (invalid). So line with slope 2 and β=-1 covers (n-1,1) only.\n\nThus many lines only cover a single point. We need lines that cover at least two points.\n\nConsider slope -1/3 (α = -1/3). For i=3, f(3) = β - 1. To be integer within [1,4], β can be 2,3,4,5. Let's try β = 4: f(i) = 4 - i/3. Then f(0)=4 (invalid), f(1)=4 - 1/3 = 3.666 (not integer), f(2)=4 - 2/3 = 3.333 (not integer), f(3)=4 - 1 = 3 (covers (n-3,3)). So L4 covers (n-3,3) only.\n\nThus many lines only cover one point.\n\nBut perhaps we can use lines that cover points across columns with different parity: e.g., slope -1/2 can cover i=0,2,4,...; slope -1/3 can cover i=0,3,6,...; slope -2/3 can cover i=0,3,...? Let's examine.\n\nGeneral rational slope α = p/q in lowest terms. Then f(i) = β + (p/q) i. For integer values at i multiples of q, we get integer values. So to have integer values at i=0 and i=2, we need denominator dividing 2. So denominator 1 or 2. Denominator 2 yields integer values at i even. So slope -1/2 yields integer values at i even (i=0,2). Similarly