constraints: Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column (i.e., per x-coordinate) because if a sunny line is not vertical, it can intersect each vertical line at most once. But we can also have horizontal lines (non-sunny) covering many points in a row.\n\nBut we need to cover all points. Let's think about covering columns.\n\nWe have columns x = 1,...,n. In column x = i, there are points (i,b) for b = 1,...,n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points.\n\nIf we use vertical lines for some columns, they cover all points in those columns. If we use horizontal lines, they cover points across rows.\n\nIf we have only two sunny lines, they are not vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. So they are \"generic\" lines with slopes not 0, ∞, -1. They can intersect each column at most one point. So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So two sunny lines can cover at most two points per column.\n\nBut each column i has n+1-i points. For columns with many points, we need non-sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines left (since total n lines). Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1.\n\nWe need to see if we can cover all points with n-2 non-sunny lines plus two sunny lines.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for all columns except maybe some columns where we use horizontal lines to cover many points.\n\nBut we have only n-2 non-sunny lines. If we use vertical lines for all columns except maybe two columns, we would need n vertical lines (one per column). But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, so we cannot have vertical lines for all columns. So we need to use some horizontal or diagonal lines to cover multiple columns.\n\nBut we need to see if it's possible.\n\nLet's try small n to test. For n=3, we have points: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1). That's 3 points. Need 3 lines. k=2? Let's see if we can have exactly 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. Non-sunny line could be vertical x=1 covering (1,1),(1,2). Then we need to cover (2,1) with 