nts. Could we cover them with non-vertical lines? Let's see: Could we cover them with a diagonal line x+y = constant? For (n-2,1) sum = n-1, (n-2,2) sum = n, (n-2,3) sum = n+1. So they are on three different diagonals. So a diagonal line cannot cover more than one of them. Could we cover them with a horizontal line? Horizontal lines have constant y. The three points have y=1,2,3, so cannot be covered by a single horizontal line. So the only way to cover all three points with a single line is vertical x=n-2. So indeed we need a vertical line covering column n-2. So one non-sunny line is forced.\n\nNow we have n-1 lines left, of which k sunny lines are allowed. For k=2, we have 2 sunny lines and n-3 non-sunny lines left. The solution then says analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). Let's examine.\n\nColumn x=n-1 has points (n-1,b) where b ranges from 1 to n+1-(n-1) = 2. So points: (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Column x=n has points (n,1) only.\n\nWe have a vertical line at x=n-2 covering (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Now we need to cover (n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1) with the remaining lines. We have k=2 sunny lines and n-3 non-sunny lines left. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal.\n\nWe could use a vertical line at x=n-1 covering (n-1,1),(n-1,2). That would be a non-sunny line. Then we would need to cover (n,1) with a sunny line. That's okay: we have 2 sunny lines allowed, we could use one for (n,1). But we also need to use the second sunny line somewhere else. But we have used only one non-sunny line (vertical x=n-2) and one non-sunny line (vertical x=n-1). That's two non-sunny lines. We have n total lines, so we have n-2 non-sunny lines used, leaving n-2 non-sunny lines? Wait we have n lines total. We used vertical x=n-2 (non-sunny). We could also use vertical x=n-1 (non-sunny). That's two non-sunny lines. The rest n-2 lines can